ce:section.ce:para:
It was once reported by Stewart et al. that C3S with different polymorphs showed different hydration performance in the early hydration, and the sequence of hydration reactivity was: M−type > T-type > R-type [53]. Zhou et al. and Staněk et al. further conducted the related studies [54–55], however, these findings were not consistent due to the different doping conditions. In this study, the isothermal calorimeter was used to investigate the effect of CaF2 and TiO2 on the hydration activity of the doped C3S (in Fig. 5
 and Fig. 6
). The hydration of C3S exhibited the typical four periods: initial period, induction period, acceleration period and deceleration period, and these periods could be observed within 24 h [40,56]. A rapid heat flow could be noticed within 0.5 h in the initial period. The sequence of heat flow from rapid to slow in the initial period would be: F3T2 > F1 > F2 > F2T0 > F3 > Control. The doped C3S showed a higher heat flow than the undoped C3S in the initial period, the increased addition of CaF2 presented a negative effect on the initial hydration of C3S (dissolution process), while the addition of TiO2 would reverse the negative effect caused by the addition of CaF2. The more addition of TiO2 would present more rapid initial hydration, and 0.5% addition of TiO2 could reduce the initial hydration of the doped C3S with 1.5% addition of CaF2. It was once reported by Katyal [37] that the presence of TiO2 up to 2.0% had an inhibiting effect on the rate of hydration C3S, but the findings of this study seems to demonstrate that the synergistic addition of CaF2 and TiO2 into C3S might enhance the dissolution of the doped C3S in the initial period hydration. The promoted initial hydration activity of the doped C3S might stem from the increased defects of the doped C3S structure [30].
